9.1  How a filesystem stores a file

TL;DR
  - A disk is a long row of blocks (usually 4 KiB). A filesystem decides which
    blocks hold which file, and keeps that map on the disk too.
  - Each file has an inode: owner, rights, times, size, and where its data
    lives, as extents ("1,200 blocks starting at block 88,000").
  - A folder is a small file listing names and inode numbers. The name is not
    part of the file itself (13.4).

      stat notes.txt              # inode, size, blocks used
      filefrag -v big.iso         # its extents on the disk

On your machine
  /         btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /home     btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /var/lib/my data btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /boot/efi vfat on /dev/nvme0n1p1
  /media/usb exfat on /dev/sda
  /home     btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2

Pitfalls
  - A 10-byte file still takes a whole block: thousands of tiny files use far
    more disk than their sizes add up to. du counts blocks, ls -l bytes.
  - Sparse files (VM images, databases) look bigger in ls -l than in du:
    unwritten parts take no space until written. Copying them carelessly can
    fill a disk.
  - Defragmenting rarely helps on SSDs, and on btrfs it un-shares blocks between
    snapshots, multiplying the space used.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 9.1 --why

